Every change to the box is an **entry** in the [archive](../../archive/), worked on in a branch of
its own and moved through a fixed series of **stages**. Each stage produces something, and ends only
when a person approves it. The work of a stage is usually done by a coding agent driven with the
`/wf-…` commands below, but moving an entry is always the person's to ask for.

## Three kinds of entry

| kind | for | stages |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **decision** | a position the project takes, argued in prose | writeup, publish |
| **milestone** | one of the box's milestones, ending in numbers | plan, build, measure, publish |
| **chore** | a refactor or tooling change with nothing to measure | plan, build, publish |

## The flow

<ModelGraph spec={workflow} />

## Working through a decision

A decision starts with `/wf-start <slug> decision` and is argued in `writeup.md`. Each question still
open is a `[!TODO]` callout in the document. The home page lists them under "On the table", and
`/wf-approve` refuses the stage until none are left.

The site is for reading; the conversation is where a decision moves. Read the entry, then answer with
`/wf-writeup`, and the document is revised with your answer. When nothing is open, `/wf-approve`
accepts it, and `/wf-publish` writes the pages that follow from it.

## What the stages guarantee

The stages are kept by a small tool, `bin/wf`, which refuses an edit the current stage does not allow
and checks the rules on every pull request.

- A branch works on one entry at a time, and merges only once that entry is done or set aside.
- An approved document is frozen until its stage is reopened, which is recorded with its reason in
  the entry's history.
- Code changes only once a plan is approved, and the pages change only at the end.
- A stage is approved only when its work is committed and complete: no open questions in a document,
  a checklist in a plan with every box ticked by the end of the build, a `## Results` section in the
  measurements, and every changed page listed by the entry.

The ledger of [open questions](../../reference/open-questions/) stays open at every stage, so that a
fact can be recorded the moment it is known.

## Going back

A stage's work sometimes shows an earlier stage wrong: a measurement contradicts the plan, or the
hardware does not match its datasheet. The work then stops, and `/wf-reopen <stage> <reason>` moves
the entry back. The reason is recorded, and every stage from there on is approved again. An entry can
also be set aside with `/wf-abandon <reason>` and taken up again with `/wf-resume <slug>`.

Once its publishing is approved, an entry is done and is never edited again. When the box later
changes what an entry built, a new entry says so, naming the one it revises.
